            <ArticleTitle>Pattern of Near Transfer Effects Following Working Memory Training With a Dual N-Back Task.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>240-252</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1027/1618-3169/a000370</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>In a randomized controlled trial, we investigated the pattern of near transfer effects of working memory (WM) training with an adaptive auditory-visuospatial dual n-back training task in healthy young adults. The results revealed significant task-specific transfer to an untrained single n-back task, and more general near transfer to a WM updating composite score plus a nearly significant effect on a composite score measuring interference control in WM. No transfer effects were seen on Active or Passive WM composites. The results are discussed in the light of cognitive versus strategy-related overlap between training and transfer tasks.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
